        <h1 class="subsection-page-title">
            WHAT ARE THE THINGS I LEARNED FROM THIS PARTICULAR TOPIC?
        </h1>

    </div>


    <div class="answer-box">

        <p>
            I learned that mathematics is not just about numbers, formulas, and solving equations. Mathematics is more than that because it can be found almost everywhere around us, especially in nature. Before learning about this topic, I mostly thought of math as something that we only use in the classroom when solving problems. However, I realized that mathematics is also present in the natural world through patterns, shapes, measurements, and arrangements.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, we can see mathematical patterns in flowers, leaves, seashells, trees, and even in the way some animals are arranged or shaped. The spiral patterns of some flowers and shells show how numbers and patterns can occur naturally. We can also observe symmetry in leaves, butterflies, flowers, and other living things. These examples helped me understand that nature follows certain patterns and that mathematics can help us describe and understand them.
        </p>

        <p>
            I also learned that mathematics is connected to our everyday lives. We can see it in objects, buildings, designs, technology, and even in the way things are organized. This made me realize that mathematics is not just about memorizing formulas or getting the correct answer. It is also a way of understanding the world around us.
        </p>

        <p>
            Overall, this topic helped me appreciate mathematics more because I learned to look at ordinary things in a different way. I now understand that mathematics is already a part of nature and our daily lives, even when we do not immediately notice it. It showed me that math is truly everywhere, and learning about it can help us understand the beauty, patterns, and order that exist in the world around us.
        </p>

    </div>


