    <div class="discussion-box">

        <p>
            For my final solution, I was able to solve the puzzle by carefully organizing all the clues and connecting them with one another. At first, the problem was difficult for me because there were many categories to consider, such as the nationality, beverage, house color, cigar brand, and pet. I had to make sure that each person, house, beverage, cigar, and pet was different. Instead of trying to solve everything at once, I worked through the clues step by step and used the information that was already given to eliminate the possibilities.
        </p>

        <p>
            I started with the clues that gave direct information. The Norwegian lives in House 1, while the center house, House 3, is occupied by the person who drinks milk. The Norwegian also lives next to the blue house, which means House 2 is blue. Since the owner of the yellow house smokes Dunhill, and the man who keeps horses lives next to the person who smokes Dunhill, I was able to connect the house color, cigar, and pet clues. I also used the clue that the Brit lives in the red house and that the green house is immediately to the left of the white house. The owner of the green house drinks coffee, which helped me determine the positions of the green and white houses.
        </p>

        <p>
            The clues involving the beverages and cigar brands were also important in completing the table. The Dane drinks tea, while the person who smokes BlueMaster drinks beer. The person who smokes Prince is the German, and the person who smokes Pall Mall keeps birds. I also considered the clues about the blends, cats, and water. The person who smokes blends lives next to the person who keeps cats and also has a neighbor who drinks water. By combining these clues instead of looking at them separately, I was able to eliminate the incorrect possibilities and fill in the remaining information.
        </p>

        <p>
            One of the biggest challenges I encountered was that some clues could only be understood after another clue had already been solved. There were times when I was unsure about where to place a particular nationality, beverage, or pet because several possibilities seemed possible. This was also the reason why I made a Trial 2. I had to review my first attempt, check the clues again, and adjust the information until all the clues were consistent with each other.
        </p>

        <p>
            I was able to solve the problem with the help of my boardmates and my roommate. They helped me understand some of the clues and the way the information could be connected. They also tried solving the puzzle themselves, so we were able to compare our answers and reasoning. Their help made the process less confusing, especially when I got stuck on some of the clues.
        </p>

        <p>
            After checking the clues and completing my final table, I found that the <strong>German owns the fish</strong>. What I learned from solving this problem is that logical reasoning is not always about immediately knowing the answer. It is about carefully examining the information, eliminating possibilities, and making sure that every conclusion is supported by the clues. The puzzle was challenging at first, but solving it step by step helped me understand how organized reasoning can be used to solve a complicated problem.
        </p>

